- Location: `.venv/` (gitignored)
- Activation: `. .venv/bin/activate` (or use `make venv`)
- Dependencies: `requirements.txt`

### Node.js Dependencies
- Location: `frontend/node_modules/` (gitignored)
- Installation: `cd frontend && npm install`
- Dependencies: `frontend/package.json`

### Environment Variables
- File: `.env` (gitignored, create from `.env.example` if provided)
- Required: `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `NOTION_API_KEY`
- Optional: `YOUR_SITE_URL`, `YOUR_SITE_NAME`

### Working Directory
- Always work from project root: `/Users/joshuafield/AIAm/`
- Use relative paths for project files
- Never create files outside project directory

# P.A.R.A. LIFE OS SPECIFIC RULES

## Terminology
- Use exact P.A.R.A. terms: **Projects**, **Areas**, **Resources**, **Archives** (capitalized)
- **Master Inbox** (not "inbox" or "main inbox")
- `para_type`, `para_link` (lowercase with underscore)
- Notion database IDs use exact property names from `notion/schemas.py`
- Claude API (not OpenAI) - use Anthropic client

## Code Style
- Python: `snake_case` for functions/variables, `PascalCase` for classes
- Type hints required for all functions
- Docstrings for public APIs only
- Maximum line length: 100 characters (Black default)
- Use `anthropic` package, not `openai`

## Testing
- All integrations require real API tests (marked `@pytest.mark.requires_api`)
- Mock only external rate limits, not API responses
- Test file structure mirrors source: `tests/notion/test_client.py`
- Mark tests with PR phase: `@pytest.mark.pr01`, `@pytest.mark.pr02`, etc.

## Git Workflow
- Feature branches: `feat/para-classifier`, `fix/notion-relation`, etc.
- Commit messages: `feat: add Claude AI processor`, `fix: handle rate limits`
- Never commit `.env` or token files
- Always show `git diff` before committing

## API Usage
- Claude API: Use `anthropic.Anthropic` client
- Default model: `claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022` or `claude-3-opus-20240229`
- Track token usage and warn at 80% budget
- Respect rate limits with exponential backoff

Acknowledge these guardrails and confirm you understand them. When working on this project, I expect you to follow these principles strictly.

